<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bartolomeo Colleoni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1395–<a href="/years/1475">1475</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bartolomeo Colleoni was an Italian condottiere from Bergamo who served both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> before becoming captain-general of the Venetian army for life in 1455. He left much of his fortune to the republic on condition that a statue of him be raised in Venice. The bronze equestrian monument by Andrea del Verrocchio, outside the church of Santi Giovanni e Paolo, is one of the masterpieces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> sculpture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bartolomeo-colleoni.jpg" alt="Bartolomeo Colleoni" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Battista Moroni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BartholomaeusColleoni1566-69.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bartolomeo Colleoni was born at Solza near Bergamo, then part of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. The Colleoni were a noble family who had been exiled with the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a> by the Visconti of Milan. His father Paolo Colleoni seized the castle of Trezzo but was assassinated by his cousins, probably on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-maria-visconti">Filippo Maria Visconti</a>, Duke of Milan. The young Colleoni trained as a soldier in the retinue of Filippo d'Arcello, the new master of Piacenza, and then served several condottieri, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braccio-da-montone">Braccio da Montone</a>, who was fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. He took the side of Alfonso of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and later that of Carmagnola.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Carmagnola was put to death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1432, Colleoni entered the direct service of the Republic of Venice, the major phase of his career. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gianfrancesco-i-gonzaga">Gianfrancesco I Gonzaga</a>, Marquess of Mantua, was nominally commander-in-chief, but Colleoni was the real leader of the army and recaptured many towns from the Milanese. When Gonzaga went over to the enemy Colleoni stayed with Venice under Gattamelata and Francesco I Sforza, winning battles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, Verona and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-garda">Lake Garda</a>.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> made peace in 1441 he joined Milan with Sforza in 1443, but the Visconti came to suspect him and imprisoned him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a> until the duke died in 1447. He then served Sforza under the Golden Ambrosian Republic and defeated the French at the Battle of Bosco Marengo. He returned to Venice in 1448, left it in 1452 when he was not elected captain-general, and was lured back in 1453 with greater pay. In 1455 he was appointed captain-general of the republic for life. He was regarded as a foremost tactician and disciplinarian, and although he often changed sides no act of treachery is imputed to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Colleoni lived at the castle of Malpaga, which he bought in 1465 and restored, and devoted himself to agricultural improvements on the estates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> had granted him and to charitable works. He died there in 1475, leaving a large sum to the republic for the Turkish war with a request that an equestrian statue of him be raised in the Piazza San Marco. Andrea del Verrocchio modelled it and Alessandro Leopardi cast it in bronze after his death. Since no monument was permitted in the piazza, it was set near the Scuola Grande of St Mark outside the Church of SS Giovanni e Paolo. In Bergamo he built himself the Cappella Colleoni, a mortuary chapel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bartolomeo Colleoni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On land the republic relied on hired condottieri such as Francesco Bussone, called Carmagnola, and <b>Bartolomeo Colleoni</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Solza, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Malpaga Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.68,9.49&amp;year=1447" aria-label="Solza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4775 554 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bartolomeo Colleoni"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5225 824h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5225 824h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.68,9.49&amp;year=1447">What was here in 1447? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
